{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "control": "D0"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 138, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a missing input-to-output residual helps far more than swapping an existing conv or pool on edge 3 for skip."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Without the 0->2 edge, node 2 receives little or no signal, so a conv on edge 2->3 adds little."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If node 1 has no direct edge to the output, a conv into node 1 contributes only indirectly or not at all."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Removing the residual skip on edge 0->3 hurts optimization more than removing a conv there."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With no direct input-to-output edge, cutting edge 2->3 removes a larger share of the paths reaching the output."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "When edge 0->2 is absent, cutting edge 0->1 can disconnect node 1 and node 2 from the input entirely."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If node 1 receives no input, a conv on edge 1->3 processes zeros and is effectively a no-op."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Filling an empty edge 3 adds a path and capacity, whereas replacing an existing skip or pool with a conv loses the residual benefit."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "If node 2 does not feed the output, removing edge 0->2 costs almost nothing."}, {"